<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A single, unified standard covering all non-monetary exchanges</div><div class="principle">ASBE No. 7 governs EXCHANGES of NON-MONETARY assets BETWEEN two PARTIES â€” WHETHER with NO monetary CONSIDERATION at ALL, or with ONLY a MINOR monetary "TOP-UP" (BOOT) PAYMENT alongside the NON-MONETARY exchange ITSELF. GENUINELY DISTINCTIVELY, this SINGLE standard COVERS ALL types of non-MONETARY assets â€” INVENTORIES, fixed ASSETS, intangible ASSETS, and LONG-term equity INVESTMENTS â€” RATHER than being SCATTERED across MULTIPLE separate ASSET-specific standards, as the EQUIVALENT guidance IS internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope â€” All Types of Non-monetary Assets</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A unified framework spanning multiple asset categories</div><div class="principle">This Standard applies to EXCHANGES INVOLVING inventories (ASBE No. 1), fixed ASSETS (ASBE No. 4), intangible ASSETS (ASBE No. 6), and LONG-term equity INVESTMENTS (ASBE No. 2) â€” WHENEVER TWO parties EXCHANGE such assets DIRECTLY, RATHER than through a CONVENTIONAL cash SALE and separate CASH purchase.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Monetary vs Non-monetary Consideration</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A quantitative threshold for the "boot" payment</div><div class="principle">An EXCHANGE QUALIFIES as a NON-MONETARY exchange (RATHER than an ORDINARY monetary SALE/purchase TRANSACTION) WHERE any MONETARY consideration ("BOOT") INVOLVED represents ONLY a MINOR PORTION of the TOTAL fair VALUE of the assets EXCHANGED â€” GENERALLY assessed AGAINST a SPECIFIED percentage THRESHOLD of the transaction's TOTAL fair value.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Commercial Substance Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Does the exchange genuinely change the entity's future cash flows?</div><div class="principle">An EXCHANGE has "COMMERCIAL SUBSTANCE" WHERE the RISK, timing, or AMOUNT of the entity's FUTURE cash FLOWS is EXPECTED to CHANGE SIGNIFICANTLY as a RESULT of the exchange â€” for EXAMPLE, where the CONFIGURATION (risk, timing, AMOUNT) of cash FLOWS from the ASSET received DIFFERS materially FROM that of the ASSET given up. This TEST DETERMINES WHICH of the TWO measurement APPROACHES applies (Sections 5 and 6).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Measurement Where Commercial Substance Exists â€” the Fair Value Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Recognising a gain or loss on exchange</div><div class="principle">WHERE the EXCHANGE HAS commercial SUBSTANCE AND the FAIR value of EITHER the asset RECEIVED or the asset GIVEN up can be MEASURED reliably, the ASSET received is MEASURED at the FAIR VALUE of the asset GIVEN UP (ADJUSTED for any BOOT paid or RECEIVED) â€” WITH the DIFFERENCE between this FAIR value and the CARRYING amount of the asset GIVEN up RECOGNISED as a GAIN or LOSS on exchange.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Measurement Where Commercial Substance Is Absent â€” the Carrying Amount Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">No gain or loss recognised</div><div class="principle">WHERE the EXCHANGE LACKS commercial SUBSTANCE, or WHERE neither asset's FAIR value can be MEASURED reliably, the ASSET received is MEASURED at the CARRYING AMOUNT of the asset GIVEN up (ADJUSTED for any BOOT paid or RECEIVED) â€” WITH NO gain or LOSS recognised on the EXCHANGE itself.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>A Genuinely Broader Standard Than Its International Counterparts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">One standard, rather than several scattered provisions</div><div class="principle">INTERNATIONALLY, the SAME general "COMMERCIAL SUBSTANCE" test APPEARS SEPARATELY within IAS 16 (fixed ASSETS) and IAS 38 (intangible ASSETS), WITHOUT a SINGLE, UNIFIED standard COVERING ALL non-MONETARY asset TYPES together (INVENTORIES exchanges, for EXAMPLE, are addressed DIFFERENTLY under IFRS 15's OWN non-cash CONSIDERATION guidance). ASBE No. 7's CHOICE to CONSOLIDATE the EQUIVALENT principles INTO a SINGLE, standalone STANDARD spanning ALL asset TYPES is a GENUINELY notable STRUCTURAL feature, EVEN though the UNDERLYING commercial-SUBSTANCE measurement LOGIC itself is BROADLY converged.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example â€” an Exchange With Commercial Substance</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognising a gain on exchange</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Swapping a delivery vehicle for a piece of production equipment</div><div class="wk-body">A company exchanges a delivery vehicle (carrying amount RMB 500,000, fair value RMB 650,000) for a piece of production equipment owned by another company, with no cash changing hands. The exchange genuinely changes the company's future cash flow profile (moving from logistics use to manufacturing use).</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the exchange HAS commercial SUBSTANCE and FAIR value is RELIABLY measurable, the company RECOGNISES the NEW equipment at RMB 650,000 (the FAIR value of the VEHICLE given up), and a GAIN on exchange of RMB 650,000 âˆ’ RMB 500,000 = <strong>RMB 150,000</strong>.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example â€” an Exchange Without Commercial Substance</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Carrying the new asset at the old asset's book value</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An exchange lacking genuine economic substance</div><div class="wk-body">Two related companies within the same group exchange similar pieces of office equipment (carrying amount RMB 200,000 each), with no meaningful change expected in either company's future cash flows.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the exchange LACKS commercial SUBSTANCE, the RECEIVING company measures the NEW equipment at the CARRYING amount of the equipment given UP â€” <strong>RMB 200,000</strong> â€” with <strong>NO gain or loss</strong> recognised on the exchange.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBE No. 7 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature and type of assets exchanged</span><span>Measurement basis applied (fair value or carrying amount), and whether commercial substance was assessed to exist</span><span>Gain or loss recognised on exchange, if any</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether an exchange has genuine commercial substance</span><span>Whether the boot payment is sufficiently minor to qualify the transaction as a non-monetary exchange</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of the assets exchanged, where the fair value model applies</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 16/38</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison â€” ASBE 7 vs IAS 16/38's non-monetary exchange provisions</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASBE 7</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 16 / IAS 38</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Commercial substance test</span><span>Same underlying logic</span><span>Same underlying logic â€” converged</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Standard structure</span><span>ONE unified standard, all asset types</span><span>Scattered across individual asset standards</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">The MEASUREMENT principle ITSELF is thoroughly CONVERGED â€” the DISTINCTIVE feature is CHINA's choice to CONSOLIDATE the equivalent GUIDANCE into a SINGLE, dedicated STANDARD.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every non-monetary exchange event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Gain/loss on exchange (commercial substance present)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised in profit or loss</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Asset received (no commercial substance)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Carried at the transferred asset's book value, no gain/loss</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 4 / No. 6</span><span>Fixed Assets / Intangible Assets â€” supply the underlying asset categories most commonly involved in non-monetary exchanges</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Commercial substance</div><div class="glossary-def">A condition met where an exchange is expected to significantly change the risk, timing, or amount of an entity's future cash flows, determining whether fair value or carrying amount measurement applies.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Boot</div><div class="glossary-def">A minor monetary payment accompanying an otherwise non-monetary asset exchange.</div></div>
</div>
</div>
